{
 "schema": 2,
 "kind": "bird-glyphs",
 "date": "2026-09-23",
 "box": 13,
 "note": "One glyph per bird, and it never changes (Eugene, 09-23: the icon animation and the second set of drawings are gone). A glyph is a list of marks in a 13-unit box centred on its own origin, drawn upright whatever the star's sway: a mark is a path `d` of absolute M, L, C, Q and Z commands only — so the page scales it by multiplying every number — stroked in the ring's line work, with `fill` either absent (an outline), `ground` (a knock-out: the page's black, so what lies behind it is hidden) or `ink` (a small solid in the ring's own colour, a pip or a spark). `tools/check.ts` holds every glyph inside the box and to those commands. Since round K15 a banded bird's levels are keyed by `band` (the index of the band in `bird-labels.json` the level draws, or a list where one level draws several), with `half` where one band is drawn by two levels (Tide's ripple and crest, the lower and upper half of open): the level turns where the word does, at the band's own threshold (`bird-glyph.ts`). Ember's levels turn where its own words do (K30): `edge` names the turn — `drums`, where the drums come on (`DRUMS_ON_ABOVE`, the one at it still without them), and `driving`, where the pulse leaves the steady band at the house's Spark — solved in `bird-glyph.ts` from `spell.ts`, never typed here; Zephyr keeps `upTo` in its own value, since its word is the plan's keys and not a band.",
